{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n3 \n \n \n1. Overview \nThe Flame Design experiment is one of six independent experiments that make up the Advanced \nCombustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME) project for which the tests were conducted within the \nCombustion Integrated Rack (CIR) on the International Space Station (ISS).   \n \nThe experiment\u2019s Principal Investigator (PI) is Prof. Richard L. Axelbaum of the Washington University of \nSt. Louis\u2019s Dept. of Energy, Environmental & Chemical Engineering.  The Co-Investigator (Co-I) is Prof. \nPeter B. Sunderland of the University of Maryland\u2019s Dept. of Fire Protection Engineering.  The ISS research \nalso benefitted greatly from the work of Phillip H. Irace who was a doctoral student at the Washington \nUniversity of St. Louis throughout the on-orbit operations. \n \nMeanwhile, Dennis P. Stocker of the NASA Glenn Research Center is the Project Scientist (PS) for ACME \nincluding Flame Design and the rest of the ACME investigations.  In ACME\u2019s preparatory drop testing, etc., \nDr. Fumiaki Takahashi of the Case Western Reserve University (CWRU) served as the Deputy Project \nScientist.  For ACME\u2019s spaceflight operations, the NASA science team included Drs. Uday G. Hegde and \nVedha Nayagam who are also with CWRU. \n \nThe ISS operations for Flame Design were conducted in two rounds of testing which occurred in April-July \n2019 and Oct. 2021 to Feb. 2022.  The two rounds occurred during Increments 59/60 and 66 and \nrespectively featured normal and inverse flames.  The inverse flame tests were conducted with the \nchamber filled with a mixture of nitrogen and ethylene (C2H4) while an oxygen/nitrogen mixture flowed \nfrom the burner.  As a result, the inverse flames were effectively inside out, e.g., where the soot formed \noutside rather than inside of the flames.  As a notable exception, the first day of testing in the second \nround was carried out in the normal flame configuration, with propane (with or without nitrogen) flowing \ninto an oxygen/nitrogen atmosphere.  The tests on that day were conducted with the long (near) \nthermocouple rake to acquire data to calibrate optical measurements of the flame temperature through \nThin Filament Pyrometry (TFP).  Meanwhile, the short (far) thermocouple rake was used on all other Flame \nDesign test days. \n \nExcept for the single propane (C3H8) test day previously mentioned, all ISS tests were conducted with the", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 3}}